few first-person shooter series have had  the longevity of far cry with the legacy  going back more than 17 years and eight  titles if you don't count far cry 6. and  despite its reputation for being a copy  paste type of series the far cry games  have gone through some drastic changes  and experiments over time all while  keeping open world chaos at the heart of  its gameplay loop in this video we're  ranking the far cry games so you know  which ones are the must plays and which  ones are the you should probably still  plays for more on far cry make sure you  keep it locked here on gamespot  i love far cry 5 like really love far  cry 5 but it's hard to deny that it  doesn't live up to its full potential  the game promises a memorable  confrontation with a cult that has  apparently strong-armed or seduced the  population of rural montana into joining  it unfortunately far cry 5 only dips its  feet into the heavy subject matter  instead of fully jumping in the  self-professed prophet joseph seed isn't  all that charismatic or compelling nor  his siblings and most of the game  consists of doing quests to take one of  them off enough so that they come and  confront you directly there are still  the typical far cry elements like  dangerous animals and goofy side  characters and i had a blast 100 in it  but ultimately far cry 5 wrote a check  it couldn't cash i did not ask for this  [Music]  this spin-off takes place in the stone  age replacing guns and explosives with  spears bows and primitive handheld  weapons and despite this it's still very  much a far cry game with the wilderness  and human enemies both posing real  threats to your survival and a mix of  assault and stealth are often necessary  to make it out alive heavily inspired by  the drug induced shangri-la missions of  far cry 4 we haven't seen anything like  far cry primal from the series since but  it proves history may be the best source  for new game settings  the original far cry is well  a far cry from later installments it  still retains the fish out of water  approach we see in so many of the later  games with jack carver finding himself  stranded on an island and battling a  seemingly unwinnable war against  mercenaries but it also goes in a more  science fiction direction the mutant  enemy elements present in the game were  abandoned for follow-ups though it does  also make it unique enough to warrant a  return trip more than 15 years after its  first release  a sequel to far cry 5 that feels just a  tad more cohesive far cry new dawn sees  the plains of montana largely reclaimed  by nature and bandits following a  nuclear apocalypse the game has quite a  bit more flair and style than its  predecessor and while its antagonists  aren't very memorable it still has a  knack for delivering short fun side  activities and never outstanding its  welcome the game also was the first in  the series to add in a few light rpg  elements that i grew to love and  appreciate  over the years far cry games have gained  a reputation for being wacky now i know  what you're thinking  where's my junk far cry 3 blood dragon a  parody slash love letter to neon soaked  80s action movies manages to be a little  bit wackier than your typical fc game  this bite-sized experience doesn't have  the same scale as far cry 3 or most of  the other entries but playing as the  ridiculously named rex power cult and  blasting everything in your path more  than makes up for that and even better  it stars michael bean who played kyle  reese in the original two terminator  movies as well as corporal hicks and  aliens  but to my video game aficionados you'll  probably know him best for the very  strong inspiration for the original  metal gear american box are  so how do you follow up one of the most  critically acclaimed first person  shooters of a generation easy you hit  the if it ain't broke don't fix it  button far cry 4 takes the action from a  warm tropical island to a cold fictional  war-torn himalayan country ruled by a  new big bad pagan men the game maintains  the structure of far cry 3 climbing  towers to expand the map clearing  outposts and hunting and gathering for  upgrades but min just isn't as memorable  as voss nor did the game provide the  same sense of surprise and discovery  that far cry 3 did  ah far cry 2 where my love for the  series began this underappreciated entry  trades the bright and colorful tropical  island of fc1 and tosses you into a  war-torn africa with malaria it was the  first mainline far cry to be developed  by ubisoft itself with crytek taking  charge of the original game and it's an  incredibly ambitious game for its time  aside from dealing with your illness  you'll also have to worry about your  weapons occasionally jamming fire  spreading and trapping you in a battle  and destructible environments rendering  your cover useless it may have lacked a  signature villain or the humor of later  games but it's an impressive achievement  nonetheless  without a doubt far cry 3 is the most  iconic game in the entire series and  quickly became the template for future  installments the game's fictional rook  islands are simply a joy to explore  despite the threat of jason brody losing  all his buddies there is wildlife to  hunt and be hunted by outposts to  liberate vehicles to joyride and of  course radio towers to climb however the  real star of the show is the villain  voss an eccentric and terrifying  criminal henchmen portrayed by actor  michael mando and his definition of  insanity monologue is most likely being  taught in a college course somewhere  future far cry games included their own  big charismatic villains but none of  them were able to hit the same notes as  vos  oh jesus  and now it's your turn hit the comment  section and let us know how you would  rank the far cry games and for more keep  it locked right here on gamespot  [Music]  you
 

